Physical annealing: a solid (e.g. a metal) is heated to a high temperature so that its particles can move freely between arrangements, and is then cooled slowly. At each temperature it is given time to reach thermal equilibrium, so as the temperature falls the particles settle into a highly ordered, low-energy (crystalline, ground) state. Cooling too fast (quenching) freezes the material in a disordered, higher-energy state. Purpose: obtain a low-energy, defect-free structure (a stronger, more stable material).
SA as optimisation (Kirkpatrick et al.):
- a state of the material ↔ a feasible solution $x$ (e.g. a TSP tour);
- its energy $E$ ↔ the objective value $f(x)$ to be minimised (e.g. tour length);
- temperature ↔ a control parameter $T$ that is lowered by a cooling schedule;
- reaching equilibrium at each temperature ↔ a Metropolis chain of trial solutions at fixed $T$;
- the ground state ↔ the global minimum; a frozen, disordered state ↔ a local minimum.
So SA is a series of Metropolis chains run at decreasing temperatures.

Network (for $n = 3$; see the diagram):
- a source $s$ with supply $n = 3$ and a sink $t$ with demand $3$ (or, without $s$ and $t$: each worker node supply $b_i = 1$, each job node demand $b_j = -1$);
- arcs $s \to W_i$, capacity 1, cost 0 (each worker does at most one job);
- arcs $W_i \to J_j$ for every worker–job pair, capacity 1, cost $c_{ij}$;
- arcs $J_j \to t$, capacity 1, cost 0 (each job is done once).
Min-cost flow model: $\min \sum_{(i,j)} c_{ij} x_{ij}$ s.t. flow balance $\sum_j x_{ij} - \sum_j x_{ji} = b_i$ at every node and $0 \le x_{ij} \le u_{ij} = 1$. Sending 3 units from $s$ to $t$ forces one unit out of every worker and into every job, which is $\sum_j x_{ij} = 1$ and $\sum_i x_{ij} = 1$: the LAP constraints. The cost of the flow is the cost of the assignment.
Integrality: supplies, demands and capacities are integers, so the basic feasible (optimal) solutions of the min-cost flow LP are integer. With capacities 1 every $x_{ij}$ is then 0 or 1, i.e. a valid assignment, without imposing $x_{ij} \in \{0, 1\}$.
